 // V18.07: Restore Article 8 text assignment (previously swallowed by a comment).
 if(viewArticles[7]) viewArticles[7].text="Before talking about how to use an inhaler properly, there's something worth understanding. Not all inhalers do the same job, and knowing the difference is just as important as knowing how to use them.\n\n🛠️ MAINTENANCE INHALERS\n\nThese are the ones we use regularly to keep our COPD under control, even on days when we're feeling reasonably well.\n\nThey usually contain long-acting bronchodilators, sometimes combined with inhaled corticosteroids, which help keep our airways open and lower the risk of flare-ups where appropriate.\n\nThink of them as preventative maintenance. Just because the engine is running smoothly doesn't mean we can stop looking after it. 😜\n\n🆘 RESCUE (EMERGENCY) INHALERS\n\nThese are our quick-relief inhalers, used when breathlessness, wheezing or chest tightness suddenly gets worse.\n\nThey contain fast-acting bronchodilators, such as salbutamol (Ventolin/albuterol), which relax the muscles around our airways and help us breathe more easily.\n\nUnlike maintenance inhalers, they're generally used when needed, although some people may be prescribed regular doses.\n\nA rescue inhaler isn't a substitute for emergency medical care. If you're struggling severely to breathe or your rescue medication isn't helping, get medical assistance.";

 // V17.93: User-approved Article 17 text only; preserve its existing image and all other content.
 if(viewArticles[16]){viewArticles[16].title="CHOOSE YOUR WORDS WISELY AND THE MIND WILL FOLLOW";viewArticles[16].text="I've noticed how much the words and labels we use shape how we live, not only with COPD but in general.\n\nNot the condition itself. That doesn't magically change because I call it something else but the way I describe my situation can change how I think about it, how I feel about it and ultimately, how I react and deal with it.\n\nA few small shifts that made a difference for me:\n\nI'm not fighting… but managing.\nI'm not limited… but have boundaries.\nI'm not short of breath… ohh fk, I am.** 😜\nI'm not declining… but fluctuating.\nI'm not dependent… but supported.\nI'm not lazy… but meditating. 🧘\nNot resting… but recovering.\nNot stuck… but pacing.\nNot giving up… but letting go.\n\nOf course, changing a few words doesn't change the reality of living with a serious illness but my words influence my thoughts. My thoughts influences my emotions and my emotions influences my actions. Ultimately, my actions influences how I experience life.\n\nFor me, the biggest change was replacing fighting (I'm a pacifist) with managing.\n\nWhen I was fighting, every bad day felt like a defeat. Every setback felt like I was losing a battle and with an illness that isn't going to disappear, that's a rather exhausting way to live but when I started managing, a bad day was simply a bad day. Something to work around, adapt to or sometimes just accept. It is as it is.\n\nI'm not pretending everything is wonderful or deny suffering not forcing myself to be positive when I feel like sh*t. Just recognising that the words I repeat to myself can eventually become the way I see myself..\n\nChoose your words wisely and the mind will follow. ❤️🤗";}
 const starts={};
