 const v1795ArticleBody=(a,i)=>{
   let body=articleBodyWithoutRepeatedTitle(a);
   if(i===8){
     const lines=body.split(/\r?\n/);
     const first=lines.findIndex(line=>line.trim());
     if(first>=0){
       const normalized=lines[first].normalize('NFKC').replace(/[^a-zA-Z ]/g,' ').replace(/\s+/g,' ').trim().toLowerCase();
       if(/^are you using you(?:r)? inhaler correctly$/.test(normalized)){
         lines.splice(first,1);
         while(first<lines.length&&!lines[first].trim())lines.splice(first,1);
         body=lines.join('\\n');
       }
     }
   }
   return body.replace(/^[\s\u00a0]+/, "");
 };
 // V18.01: Article 9 approved text, real paragraph breaks (not literal backslash-n).
 if(viewArticles[8]) viewArticles[8].text="One little puff. So many ways to get it wrong. 😜\n\nHere's something worth thinking about. Studies suggest that more than half of people using inhalers make at least one mistake in their technique. And considering how much we depend on these little devices to keep us breathing, that's rather worrying.\n\nI've been using inhalers for years, and one thing I've learned is that having the right medication is only half the story. Getting it into the lungs, where it's actually supposed to do its job, is just as important. Otherwise, we're essentially giving our tongue and throat some rather expensive medication while our lungs wait patiently for their share. 😉\n\n🫁 Not all inhalers work the same way\n\nThis is probably the most important thing to understand. With a conventional pressurised inhaler, such as Ventolin, the idea is to breathe in slowly and steadily while pressing the canister. But with a dry-powder inhaler, the technique is different. You generally need a quicker, stronger inhalation to draw the medication into your lungs.\n\nAnd then there are soft-mist inhalers, breath-actuated devices and spacers. So there isn't one universal technique that works for every inhaler.\n\n😤 The little mistakes that make a big difference\n\nOne common mistake is failing to breathe out before taking a dose. Ideally, we breathe out gently, away from the mouthpiece, before inhaling the medication. No need to force every last molecule of air out of our already struggling lungs.\n\nAnother is poor coordination. With a conventional pressurised inhaler, pressing the canister too early or too late can mean much of the medication ends up in the mouth or throat rather than the lungs. That's where a spacer can be particularly helpful. It makes coordination easier and can improve medication delivery with compatible inhalers.\n\nThen there's holding our breath afterwards. Ideally, we try for up to ten seconds, or as long as comfortably possible. With severe COPD, ten seconds can sometimes feel like an eternity, so there's no point turning blue trying to impress the inhaler. 😜\n\nAnd let's not forget cleaning. Some inhalers need regular mouthpiece cleaning to prevent blockages, while others must be kept dry. Always follow the instructions for the particular device.\n\n💊 Rescue inhalers and maintenance inhalers\n\nAnother important distinction. Rescue inhalers are intended to provide relatively quick relief when symptoms suddenly worsen. Maintenance inhalers, on the other hand, are generally prescribed for regular use, even on days when we're feeling reasonably well.\n\nSkipping maintenance medication simply because we're having a good day can undermine the treatment plan. And taking more rescue medication than prescribed isn't necessarily the answer when breathing becomes difficult.\n\n🔍 A few things I always consider\n\nBefore using an inhaler, it's worth checking that it's the correct medication, that there's medication left, that the device hasn't expired and that it has been prepared according to its instructions.\n\nSome pressurised inhalers need shaking, others don't. Some require priming after a period without use. Dry-powder inhalers have their own preparation procedures. If more than one puff is prescribed, follow the recommended interval for that particular inhaler.\n\nAnd if you're using an inhaler containing corticosteroids, rinsing your mouth and spitting afterwards helps reduce the risk of oral thrush.\n\n❤️ My point?\n\nWe spend so much time worrying about oxygen levels, lung function, medication and the progression of COPD that it's surprisingly easy to overlook something as basic as how we actually use our inhalers.\n\nA simple technique check with a doctor, nurse or pharmacist can sometimes make a real difference. Ask them to watch you use your own device. Not just explain it, but actually watch.\n\nAfter all, there's not much point spending money on medication if half of it ends up decorating the back of your throat.\n\nThe right medication, delivered the wrong way, may not give us the benefit we're expecting, and with COPD, every little bit of effective treatment matters.\n\nTake care of those lungs, my friends. They're the only pair we've got. ❤️🤗\n\nJust sharing what I've learned from living with severe emphysema. Not medical advice. Different inhalers require different techniques, so always follow your own device instructions and prescribed treatment plan.";
